{
	title: 'Card headers wrap to the left edge',
	spec: "Every card header, web and terminal, flows its time and text as one paragraph: a wrapped header continues at the left edge, under the time, never in a hanging indent or a narrow column. Web: CardHeader is the one header layout: a .flow paragraph of stamp, icon, label and inline extras, then a .side group (actions such as edit and discard, status, activity, the block link) at the right of the first line. .side follows the title in reading and focus order and is absolutely placed; a header float spacer sized to it by one shared ResizeObserver (--side-width, --side-height) shortens only the lines beside it, so later title lines use the full width. An open card's diff stats take their own row under the title. A folded header stays one row with an ellipsis. Inline result headers keep their stamp and status columns. Terminal: itemView.headed wraps the title with ansi.wrap instead of clipping it; thinking puts its time before the first paragraph, or on its own row before a heading, list or code. Tests: at phone width a wrapped card header and a wrapped question continue at the time's left edge; open EDIT titles clear the block link.",
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'First done for questions only (a flow variant beside the hanging grid); the user rejected the scope: one layout for every header, in one place per client.',
		'Title beside right columns wrapped into a narrow column on phones (one word per line under a long status); the user asked for one short first line and full-width later lines. Floating .side before .flow broke focus and reading order (question Shift+Tab test), hence the absolutely placed .side plus a measured float spacer. ResizeObserver runs before paint, so the spacer never flashes.'
